#+AUTHOR: Shaul
#+STARTUP: overview num
#+OPTIONS: toc:3

* Introduction

This is a comprehensive guide to a literate Emacs configuration optimized for:

- *Hebrew (RTL) as default*, with seamless switching to English (LTR)
- *Academic document preparation* with LaTeX (LuaLaTeX + Babel), ConTeXt, and Typst
- *Unlimited structure depth* in headings and lists (Org and LaTeX)
- *Nested footnotes* across all three document systems
- *Org-roam* for Zettelkasten-style note-taking with full Hebrew support
- *Seforim library management* for fast searching of large Hebrew text collections
- *PDF annotation* with org-noter integration
- *Bibliography management* with Citar

The configuration is written in literate Org format (=config.org=) and automatically tangles to =config.el= on save.

** System Requirements

This configuration runs on NixOS with a dedicated =emacs.nix= module. The following are provided by the NixOS configuration:

| Category | Packages |
|----------|----------|
| Emacs | =emacs-pgtk= (native Wayland, built-in =use-package=) |
| Nix-provided Emacs packages | =jinx=, =exec-path-from-shell=, =pdf-tools= (use =:ensure nil=) |
| TeX | =texlive.combined.scheme-full= (includes LuaLaTeX, ConTeXt) |
| Typst | =typst=, =tinymist= (LSP server) |
| Fonts | =culmus= (Hebrew: David CLM, Nachlieli CLM, Miriam Mono CLM), =jetbrains-mono=, =noto-fonts= |
| Search | =plocate=, =recoll=, =ripgrep=, =ripgrep-all=, =fd= |
| Spell | =enchant2=, =hunspell= with =en_US= and =he_IL= dictionaries |
| PDF | =poppler=, =poppler-utils= (for pdf-tools and recoll) |
| Document converters | =pandoc=, =antiword=, =catdoc=, =unzip=, =calibre= |
| Dev | =python3=, =python-lsp-server=, =rustc=, =cargo=, =rust-analyzer=, =jdk=, =jdt-language-server= |
| VCS | =git= |
| Other | =sqlite= (org-roam), =graphviz= (org-roam graph), =xapian= (recoll) |
| Icons | Run =M-x all-the-icons-install-fonts= after first launch |

*** NixOS Module

The Emacs environment is defined in =emacs.nix=. Import it from =configuration.nix=:

#+begin_example
imports = [ ./emacs.nix ];
#+end_example

The module provides:
- All system packages and fonts
- Emacs with Nix-provided packages (jinx, exec-path-from-shell, pdf-tools)
- Plocate service with hourly indexing
- Recoll configuration and systemd timer for periodic indexing
- User membership in =plocate= group
- Environment variables (=DICPATH=, =OSFONTDIR=)
- Seforim directory creation

*** Important: Nix-Provided vs MELPA Packages

Packages provided by Nix must use =:ensure nil= in =config.org=:

| Package | Source | =:ensure= |
|---------|--------|-----------|
| =jinx= | Nix | =nil= |
| =exec-path-from-shell= | Nix | =nil= |
| =pdf-tools= | Nix | =nil= |
| Everything else | MELPA | =t= (default) |

** File Locations

| File | Location |
|------|----------|
| NixOS Emacs module | =/etc/nixos/emacs.nix= (or your flake path) |
| Emacs config (source) | =~/.emacs.d/config.org= |
| Emacs config (tangled) | =~/.emacs.d/config.el= |
| Init file | =~/.emacs.d/init.el= |
| Org directory | =~/Documents/org/= |
| Org-roam notes | =~/Documents/roam/= |
| Roam daily notes | =~/Documents/roam/daily/= |
| Bibliography | =~/Documents/bibliography.bib= |
| Seforim library | =~/Documents/seforim/= |
| Undo history | =~/.emacs.d/undo-tree-history/= |
| Recoll config | =~/.recoll/recoll.conf= (managed by NixOS) |
| Plocate database | =/var/cache/locatedb= |

** First Run Tasks

After first launch:

1. Wait for MELPA packages to install (first startup is slow)

2. Install icon fonts:
   : M-x all-the-icons-install-fonts RET

3. Initialize org-roam database:
   : M-x org-roam-db-sync RET

4. Build Seforim indexes (if using Seforim library):
   : M-x seforim-reindex RET

5. Verify tools are available:
   : M-x seforim-status RET

Note: =pdf-tools= is installed via Nix and does not need =M-x pdf-tools-install=. It runs =pdf-tools-install :no-query= automatically.

** Verifying the Installation

After =sudo nixos-rebuild switch= and starting Emacs:

#+begin_src shell
# Verify plocate group membership (re-login after first rebuild)
groups | grep plocate

# Verify recoll config
cat ~/.recoll/recoll.conf

# Verify fonts
fc-list | grep -i \"david\\|nachlieli\\|miriam\"

# Verify tools
which plocate fd rg rga recoll typst tinymist lualatex
#+end_src

In Emacs:

#+begin_example
M-x jinx-languages RET        → should show \"en he\"
M-x seforim-status RET        → should show all tools ✓
C-h v exec-path RET           → should include /run/current-system/sw/bin
#+end_example

* Basic Emacs Usage

** Key Notation

| Notation | Meaning |
|----------|---------|
| =C-= | Hold Control key |
| =M-= | Hold Alt/Meta key |
| =S-= | Hold Shift key |
| =RET= | Press Enter |
| =SPC= | Press Space |
| =TAB= | Press Tab |

Example: =C-x C-f= means \"hold Control, press x, release, hold Control, press f\".

** Essential Commands

| Keybinding | Description |
|------------|-------------|
| =C-x C-f= | Open/create file |
| =C-x C-s= | Save file |
| =C-x C-c= | Quit Emacs |
| =C-x b= | Switch buffer (with consult) |
| =C-x C-b= | List buffers (ibuffer) |
| =C-x k= | Close buffer |
| =C-g= | Cancel current command |
| =M-x= | Run any command by name |
| =C-h k= | Describe what a keybinding does |
| =C-h f= | Describe a function |
| =C-h v= | Describe a variable |
| =C-h m= | Describe current mode |

** Navigation

| Keybinding | Description |
|------------|-------------|
| =C-f= / =C-b= | Forward/backward one character |
| =M-f= / =M-b= | Forward/backward one word |
| =C-n= / =C-p= | Next/previous line |
| =C-a= / =C-e= | Beginning/end of line |
| =M-<= / =M->= | Beginning/end of buffer |
| =C-v= / =M-v= | Page down/up |
| =C-s= | Search in buffer (consult-line) |
| =M-g g= | Go to line number |

** Editing

| Keybinding | Description |
|------------|-------------|
| =C-d= | Delete character forward |
| =M-d= | Delete word forward |
| =C-k= | Kill (cut) to end of line |
| =C-w= | Kill (cut) region |
| =M-w= | Copy region |
| =C-y= | Yank (paste) |
| =M-y= | Cycle through kill ring (after =C-y=) |
| =C-/= | Undo |
| =C-?= | Redo |
| =C-x u= | Visual undo tree |
| =C-SPC= | Set mark (start selection) |
| =C-x h= | Select entire buffer |

** Windows

| Keybinding | Description |
|------------|-------------|
| =C-x 2= | Split horizontally (and follow) |
| =C-x 3= | Split vertically (and follow) |
| =C-x 0= | Close current window |
| =C-x 1= | Close all other windows |
| =M-o= | Switch window (ace-window) |
| =C-c <left>= | Undo window layout change |
| =C-c <right>= | Redo window layout change |

** Getting Help

| Keybinding | Description |
|------------|-------------|
| =C-h r= | Open Emacs manual |
| =C-h k= | Describe keybinding |
| =C-h f= | Describe function |
| =C-h v= | Describe variable |
| =C-h a= | Search for command (apropos) |
| =C-h m= | Describe current major mode |
| =C-h b= | List all keybindings |
| =C-h F= | Describe function (helpful) |
| =C-h C= | Describe command (helpful) |

After pressing any prefix key (like =C-c= or =C-x=), wait briefly and which-key will show available completions.

* Undo and Redo

This configuration uses =undo-tree= for visual, persistent undo/redo that survives between sessions.

** Basic Undo/Redo

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-/= | Undo |
| =C-?= | Redo |
| =C-x u= | Open visual undo tree |

** Visual Undo Tree

Press =C-x u= to open a graphical visualization of your undo history:

#+begin_example
                    o  (current state)
                    |
                    o  (last change)
                   /|
                  o o  (branching history)
                  |
                  o  (earlier state)
#+end_example

*** Navigation in Undo Tree

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| =p= / =n= | Move up/down (earlier/later) |
| =b= / =f= | Switch branches |
| =t= | Toggle timestamps |
| =d= | Toggle diff display |
| =q= | Quit undo tree |

** Persistent Undo

Undo history is automatically saved to =~/.emacs.d/undo-tree-history/= and restored when you reopen files. This means you can undo changes from previous sessions.

The configuration maintains large undo limits:
- 80MB undo limit
- 120MB strong limit
- 300MB outer limit

* Focused Writing Mode

Focused writing mode centers your text in an 80-column window, removing distractions.

** Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c w= | Toggle focused writing mode |
| =C-c W= | Set custom column width |

** What It Does

When enabled:
- Text is soft-wrapped at 80 columns (no hard line breaks inserted)
- Content is visually centered in the window
- Ideal for prose writing in Org, LaTeX, or Markdown

When disabled:
- Returns to normal full-width editing

** Customizing Width

Press =C-c W= and enter a number to change the column width. Common values:
- =60= - Narrow, very focused
- =80= - Standard (default)
- =100= - Wider, good for code-heavy documents
- =120= - Wide, approaching full screen

* Hebrew and RTL Support

** How Direction Works

The configuration automatically sets text direction based on major mode:

| Mode Type | Default Direction |
|-----------|-------------------|
| Org, LaTeX, ConTeXt, Typst | RTL (Hebrew) |
| Text, Markdown | RTL (Hebrew) |
| Programming modes | LTR (English) |

You can always override manually.

** Switching Languages

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =F9= | Toggle input method (Hebrew ↔ English keyboard) |
| =C-c d= | Toggle text direction (RTL ↔ LTR) |
| =C-c r= | Force RTL direction |
| =C-c l= | Force LTR direction |
| =C-c D= | Open direction controls hydra menu |

** Visual Indicators

The modeline shows current state:
- =[RTL עב]= — Hebrew mode, right-to-left
- =[LTR EN]= — English mode, left-to-right

The indicator updates only when direction changes (no performance cost).

** Direction Controls Hydra (=C-c D=)

#+begin_example
Direction Controls

r: RTL (Hebrew)    l: LTR (English)    t: Toggle direction
h: Hebrew spell    e: English spell    b: Both languages
B: Toggle bidi     w: Focus writing    W: Set width
q: quit
#+end_example

** Performance Toggle

For very large files (>5MB), expensive features are automatically disabled:
- Jinx spell checking turned off
- Bidi reordering disabled

For manual control:

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c B= | Toggle bidi reordering on/off |

Re-enable when you need proper RTL display.

** Best Practices

1. *Hebrew documents*: Just start typing. Org, LaTeX, etc. default to RTL.
2. *English documents*: Press =C-c l= to switch to LTR.
3. *Mixed documents*: Use =C-c d= to toggle as needed, or use language environments in LaTeX.
4. *Code*: Automatically LTR in all programming modes.

* Spell Checking

Spell checking uses Jinx with Enchant2 backend, supporting both English and Hebrew simultaneously.

** Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =M-$= | Correct word at point (jinx-correct) |

** Spell Language Switching

Available via the Direction Controls hydra (=C-c D=):

| Key in hydra | Action |
|--------------|--------|
| =h= | Switch to Hebrew only |
| =e= | Switch to English only |
| =b= | Use both English + Hebrew |

Or call directly:

| Command | Action |
|---------|--------|
| =M-x my/spell-hebrew= | Hebrew dictionary only |
| =M-x my/spell-english= | English dictionary only |
| =M-x my/spell-both= | Both dictionaries |

** Where Spell Checking is Active

Jinx is automatically enabled in:
- =text-mode= (and derived modes)
- =org-mode=
- =LaTeX-mode=

It is automatically disabled in buffers larger than 5MB for performance.

** NixOS Requirements

Spell checking requires these packages (provided by =emacs.nix=):
- =enchant2= — spell checking library
- =hunspell= with =en_US= and =he_IL= dictionaries
- =jinx= — Emacs package (provided via Nix, use =:ensure nil=)

The =DICPATH= environment variable is set by =emacs.nix= to point to the Nix-provided dictionaries.

* Org Mode

** Basic Structure

#+begin_example
,* Heading Level 1
,** Heading Level 2
,*** Heading Level 3 (unlimited levels)
,**** Level 4
,***** Level 5
,****** Level 6 (and so on...)

Regular paragraph text.

- Unordered list item
  - Nested item (unlimited nesting)
    - Even more nested
      - And more...

1. Ordered list item
   1. Nested numbered item
      1. More nesting
         1. Unlimited levels
#+end_example

** Headings

*** Syntax

| Syntax | Level |
|--------|-------|
| =*= | Level 1 |
| =**= | Level 2 |
| =***= | Level 3 |
| =****= | Level 4 |
| ... | (no limit) |

This configuration supports unlimited heading depth, both in Org and when exporting to LaTeX.

*** Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =M-RET= | Insert heading at same level |
| =M-S-RET= | Insert TODO heading |
| =M-LEFT= / =M-RIGHT= | Promote/demote heading |
| =M-UP= / =M-DOWN= | Move heading up/down |
| =TAB= | Cycle visibility (folded → children → all) |
| =S-TAB= | Cycle global visibility |
| =C-c C-n= / =C-c C-p= | Next/previous heading |
| =C-c C-u= | Go to parent heading |

** Lists

*** Unordered Lists

#+begin_example
- Item 1
  - Sub-item (indent 2 spaces)
    - Sub-sub-item
      - And so on... (unlimited)
+ Alternative bullet
  + Sub-item
#+end_example

*** Ordered Lists

#+begin_example
1. First item
   1. Sub-item
      1. Sub-sub-item
         1. Unlimited nesting
2. Second item
   a. Alphabetical sub-item
      i. Roman numeral sub-item
#+end_example

*** Checkboxes

#+begin_example
- [ ] Unchecked item
- [X] Checked item
- [-] Partially done (some sub-items checked)
#+end_example

*** List Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =M-RET= | Insert new list item |
| =M-LEFT= / =M-RIGHT= | Change indent level |
| =M-UP= / =M-DOWN= | Move item up/down |
| =C-c -= | Cycle bullet style (-, +, *, 1., etc.) |
| =C-c C-c= | Toggle checkbox |
| =S-LEFT= / =S-RIGHT= | Cycle checkbox state |

** Text Formatting

| Syntax | Result |
|--------|--------|
| =*bold*= | *bold* |
| =/italic/= | /italic/ |
| =_underline_= | _underline_ |
| =~code~= | ~code~ |
| =+strikethrough+= | +strikethrough+ |
| ~=verbatim=~ | =verbatim= |

** Links

*** Syntax

#+begin_example
[[https://example.com][Link text]]
[[file:./other-file.org][Internal file link]]
[[#custom-id][Link to heading with CUSTOM_ID]]
[[*Heading Name][Link to heading by name]]
#+end_example

*** Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c C-l= | Insert or edit link |
| =C-c C-o= | Open link at point |
| =RET= | Open link (org-return-follows-link enabled) |

** Footnotes

*** Syntax

#+begin_example
This text has a footnote[fn:1].

Another footnote[fn:custom-name].

[fn:1] This is the footnote content.

[fn:custom-name] Named footnotes work too.
#+end_example

*** Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c C-x f= | Insert new footnote |
| =C-c C-c= | Jump between footnote reference and definition |

*** Hebrew Footnote RTL Fix

Hebrew footnotes can display as LTR because =[fn:N]= starts with LTR characters. The configuration automatically inserts a Right-to-Left Mark when creating new footnotes.

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c h f= | Fix RTL in all existing footnotes in buffer |

